All rights reserved.

This directory is NOT covered by the MIT/Apache-2.0 licenses that apply to the
source code of this repository. Those licenses cover `src/`, `crates/`, and the
build tooling; they stop at this directory.

"Assets" means every file under `assets/` — the textures under
`assets/textures/`, the models under `assets/models/`, and the content tables
(`blocks.toml`, `items.toml`, `entities.toml`, `worldgen.toml`, `recipes.toml`,
`spawning.toml`).

WHAT YOU MAY DO

  - Use these assets to build, run, modify and test this software, including a
    fork of it, for your own personal or development purposes.
  - Reproduce them incidentally where the software itself is reproduced — a
    clone of the repository, a build artifact, a screenshot, a video.

WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

  - Redistribute the assets on their own, or as part of another product, game,
    resource pack, or asset pack.
  - Use them in a work that presents itself as Wyvencraft, or that implies an
    association with Wyvencraft. See TRADEMARK.md.
  - Sell them, or sell access to a product whose distinguishing appearance is
    these assets.

The engine — the nine `wyven-*` crates — carries no assets and is licensed
permissively. Building something of your own on it is expressly encouraged;
bring your own art.

PROVENANCE

These assets are original work. They follow Minecraft's conventions — canvas
sizes, sheet layouts, UV unwraps and file-naming — because the software reads
and writes those formats, and a model's unwrap is determined by its geometry.
Conventions and layouts are interoperability facts; the artwork itself is not
copied.

That was verified rather than assumed. Every tracked PNG was decoded and
compared against all 3,392 textures shipped in Minecraft 1.21.8, at native size
and at every integer nearest-neighbour downsample — the transform an upscaled
copy would have gone through. No file matched any of them, and in the 59
directed comparisons against the same-named Minecraft texture, not one shared a
single exact pixel value. The mob sheets do share their *silhouette* with
Minecraft's at 98-99%, which is expected and not evidence of copying: a skin's
opaque region is the model's UV unwrap, and these models deliberately use the
same box layout so that a sheet authored for one reads correctly on the other.
The colours within that silhouette differ throughout.

This compares against Minecraft 1.21.8. It is evidence about that release, not
a warranty against every possible source.

For third-party notices covering the compiled dependencies of a distributed
binary, see the build's THIRD-PARTY notices rather than this file.
